# minimum qore version
%requires qore >= 2.0
%requires xml
%requires WSDL
%requires Util >= 3.0
%requires Logger
%requires HttpClientIo
%requires HttpUtil
%requires(reexport) ConnectionProvider >= 1.4

%modern

module SoapClientIo {
    version = "1.0";
    desc = "SOAP client module with async I/O via HttpClientIo";
    author = "David Nichols <david@qore.org>";
    url = "http://qore.org";
    license = "MIT";
    init = sub () {
        ConnectionSchemeCache::registerScheme("soapio", SoapIoConnection::ConnectionScheme);
        ConnectionSchemeCache::registerScheme("soapios", SoapIoConnection::ConnectionScheme);
    };
}

/** @mainpage SoapClientIo Module

    @tableofcontents

    @section soapclientiointro SoapClientIo Module Introduction

    The %SoapClientIo module provides a SOAP client implementation using async I/O via
    \c HttpClientIo::HttpClientConnectionManager with automatic protocol negotiation
    (HTTP/3 &rarr; HTTP/2 &rarr; HTTP/1.1) and connection pooling.

    This module is the async I/O counterpart to the \c SoapClient module. It reuses the same
    \c WSDL web-service model, message serialization, envelope validation, node processing and
    fault grammar; only the transport differs. \c SoapClient remains available and unchanged for
    code that needs the synchronous \c HTTPClient API.

    To use this module, use \c "%requires SoapClientIo" in your code.

    All the public symbols in the module are defined in the SoapClientIo namespace.

    The module provides the following classes:
    - @ref SoapClientIo::SoapClientIo "SoapClientIo" - the main SOAP client class
    - @ref SoapClientIo::SoapIoConnection "SoapIoConnection" - connection provider for the
      ConnectionProvider framework

    @subsection soapclientio_features Features

    - SOAP 1.1 and SOAP 1.2 operations over async I/O with connection pooling and automatic
      protocol negotiation (HTTP/3 &rarr; HTTP/2 &rarr; HTTP/1.1)
    - The same WSDL model, request serialization, response decoding, envelope validation,
      mandatory-header processing and fault grammar as the \c SoapClient module
    - Native and retained XML response values, and portable type preservation
    - MTOM/XOP requests with the \c mtom option, also as a SoapIoConnection option, and MTOM/XOP response
      decoding
    - SOAP 1.2 envelope-free response retrieval with @ref SoapClientIo::SoapClientIo::getSoapResponse()
    - WS-Addressing 1.0 requests and response checks, as described for the \c SoapClient module, with the
      \c addressing, \c addressing_must_understand and \c reply_receiver options and the \c addressing call
      option
    - ConnectionProvider integration with \c soapio:// and \c soapios:// URL schemes

    @subsection soapclientio_urls Endpoint URLs and Operation References

    The selected WSDL port supplies the complete endpoint URL unless the constructor's \c url option
    overrides it. A missing path becomes <tt>/</tt>; an explicit path, trailing slash, query (including
    an empty query), and percent escapes are retained. Fragments are removed and unsafe characters are
    encoded. SOAP bindings send their envelope to this fixed endpoint; no operation name is appended.

    WSDL HTTP bindings instead resolve their serialized operation URI reference against the complete
    endpoint with \c resolve_url() and \c RESOLVE_URL_ENCODE | RESOLVE_URL_NO_FRAGMENT. The same rules
    apply to the resource reference of \c getSoapResponse(). WSDL HTTP operation locations must be relative
    references (including absolute paths and authority-relative references); a scheme-bearing absolute URL
    is rejected by the WSDL model. The absolute-URL row below applies to \c getSoapResponse().
    Resolution is per call and never changes
    \c getUrl(), the configured headers, or another concurrent call's destination.

    |!Endpoint|!Reference|!Resolved request URL
    |<tt>https://api.example.com/base/</tt>|<tt>send</tt>|<tt>https://api.example.com/base/send</tt>
    |<tt>https://api.example.com/base/file</tt>|<tt>send</tt>|<tt>https://api.example.com/base/send</tt>
    |<tt>https://api.example.com/base/file</tt>|<tt>/send</tt>|<tt>https://api.example.com/send</tt>
    |<tt>https://api.example.com/base/dir/</tt>|<tt>../send</tt>|<tt>https://api.example.com/base/send</tt>
    |<tt>https://api.example.com/base/file?old=1</tt>|<tt>?rev=2</tt>|<tt>https://api.example.com/base/file?rev=2</tt>
    |<tt>https://api.example.com/base/file?old=1</tt>|empty or <tt>#help</tt>|<tt>https://api.example.com/base/file?old=1</tt>
    |<tt>https://api.example.com/base/file?old=1</tt>|<tt>?</tt>|<tt>https://api.example.com/base/file?</tt>
    |<tt>https://api.example.com/base/</tt>|<tt>//other.example.com/send</tt>|<tt>https://other.example.com/send</tt>
    |<tt>https://api.example.com/base/</tt>|<tt>http://other.example.com/send</tt>|<tt>http://other.example.com/send</tt>

    HTTP-binding URL replacement and query serialization happen before resolution. Encoded slashes and
    encoded dot segments remain data. Literal dot segments in references are resolved. Generated GET
    parameters replace an inherited base query for an empty operation reference; a zero-part input with
    an empty reference retains the base query. REST API-root appending is never applied here.

    Default \c Authorization, \c Cookie, and \c Host headers are removed for a different origin
    (scheme, host, effective port). Header names and DNS hosts are compared without regard to case;
    UNIX socket paths remain case-sensitive. Explicit per-call \c http_header values belong to the
    selected target and replace defaults case-insensitively. User information in an operation/resource
    reference is ignored for authentication. Constructor username/password options take precedence over
    endpoint user information and produce a Basic authorization default scoped to that endpoint's origin.
    Redirects then follow the connection manager's origin and credential rules.

    These request-target rules are independent of HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2, and HTTP/3. \c SoapClient uses the same
    URI resolution for HTTP bindings and SOAP-response retrieval, through \c HTTPClient::sendUrl(); its
    released SOAP-binding transport and constructor credential precedence remain unchanged.

    @subsection soapclientio_usage Usage Example

    @code{.py}
%modern
%requires SoapClientIo

SoapClientIo client({"wsdl": "http://example.com/service?wsdl"});
auto result = client.callOperation("echo", {"body": "invoice-71"});
printf("result: %N\n", result);
client.close();
    @endcode

    @section soapclientio_relnotes Release Notes

    See @subpage soapclientioreleasenotes.
*/

